    {
      "name": "rb_1",
      "first": 0,
      "second": 4,
      "reasoning": "FIRST gives generic controller instructions without mentioning either Vive or Index. SECOND explicitly provides separate Vive and Index instructions for both attack scenarios, although its Index button labels are nonstandard.",
      "evidence": "Vive: Press the trigger button on the right controller to grab the weapon.\n   - Index: Press and hold L2 to grab the weapon."
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_2",
      "first": 1,
      "second": 4,
      "reasoning": "FIRST offers trigger-based ranged attacks, but its melee scheme detects swings and maps different swing motions to attack types, substantially relying on the approach the user wanted to avoid. SECOND activates attacks with buttons and uses controller movement for positioning and aiming rather than recognizing attack gestures.",
      "evidence": "Different swing motions (e.g., horizontal, vertical, thrusting) could be mapped to different attack types or combos."
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_3",
      "first": 4,
      "second": 3,
      "reasoning": "FIRST specifies physically swinging the controller to produce a melee strike. SECOND specifies moving the right hand toward the enemy and pressing an attack input, but its nonstandard Index R2 labeling makes that hardware mapping less precise.",
      "evidence": "Vive: Move the right hand towards the enemy, then press the trigger button again to perform the attack."
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_4",
      "first": 4,
      "second": 3,
      "reasoning": "FIRST supplies a complete hold-trigger, aim, release-trigger ranged input. SECOND supplies aiming and firing inputs, but reuses the same press-and-hold Index input for both entering aim and firing without explaining the transition.",
      "evidence": "Releasing the trigger would then cause the avatar to throw/release the ranged weapon in the aimed direction."
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_5",
      "first": 2,
      "second": 1,
      "reasoning": "FIRST explicitly maps controller motion to an avatar's weapon, providing some third-person framing, but never explains the relationship to third-person movement and viewpoint. SECOND discusses VR motion controls but describes physically walking to the enemy and directly handling weapons without establishing the remotely controlled third-person avatar.",
      "evidence": "Approach the enemy by physically walking towards them in the VR space."
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_6",
      "first": 3,
      "second": 2,
      "reasoning": "FIRST explains the main motion-to-strike and trigger-to-projectile mechanics clearly, although its optional bow example leaves drawing and release coordination unclear. SECOND describes an interaction sequence, but nonstandard Index labels, unspecified effects of dragging the weapon, and the repeated aim/fire hold input leave substantial mechanical ambiguity.",
      "evidence": "Index: Move the right hand towards the enemy, then press and hold R2 to bring up the aiming reticle."
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_7",
      "first": 4,
      "second": 3,
      "reasoning": "FIRST specifies physical controller interactions for both melee and ranged attacks. SECOND also specifies hand movements and button inputs for both scenarios, but its Index inputs are expressed using nonstandard L2/R2 labels rather than clearly identified controller controls.",
      "evidence": "As an enemy approaches within melee range, the player physically swings the controller(s) in the desired attack direction."
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_8",
      "first": 4,
      "second": 4,
      "reasoning": "Both schemes incorporate substantial physical interaction. FIRST uses controller swings, physical aiming, drawing, and blocking. SECOND uses grabbing, hand-directed weapon positioning, motion-based aiming, and pulling the hand back to zoom.",
      "evidence": "Adjust the aiming reticle by moving the right hand slightly to the left or right, and then pulling the right hand back to zoom in."
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_9",
      "first": 2,
      "second": 4,
      "reasoning": "FIRST clearly separates melee and outside-melee examples but provides no Vive/Index distinction. SECOND uses separate range headings, numbered steps, and labeled Vive/Index bullets.",
      "evidence": "Outside Melee Range:"
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_10",
      "first": 1,
      "second": 4,
      "reasoning": "FIRST initially describes direct motion mapping, which need not involve gesture classification, but then explicitly proposes mapping horizontal, vertical, and thrusting swings to attack types or combos. SECOND uses button-triggered attacks and does not propose recognizing complex predefined attack gestures; its reference to a drawing gesture is not an attack-recognition mechanic.",
      "evidence": "Different swing motions (e.g., horizontal, vertical, thrusting) could be mapped to different attack types or combos."
    }
  ]
}

