<first_data>
Classic lateral thinking puzzle!

Here's the solution:

I would ask one of the guards, "If I were to ask the other guard which direction leads to freedom, what would he say?"

Think about it for a moment...

If I ask the truth-telling guard, he will tell me what the liar would say, which would be the opposite of the truth. So, if the truth-telling guard says the right direction leads to freedom, it actually means the left direction leads to freedom. And if the truth-telling guard says the left direction leads to freedom, it actually means the right direction leads to freedom.

If I ask the liar, he will lie about what the truth-telling guard would say. So, if the liar says the right direction leads to freedom, it actually means the left direction leads to freedom. And if the liar says the left direction leads to freedom, it actually means the right direction leads to freedom.

Either way, by asking this question, I can determine the correct direction to freedom with certainty!
</first_data>

<second_data>
To determine which guard is telling the truth and which is lying, you can ask one of the guards a question. The key is to ask a question in such a way that it doesn't matter which guard you ask; the answer will still be informative.

Here's the question you can ask: "If I were to ask the other guard which door leads to freedom, what would he say?"

There are two possible scenarios:

1. If you ask the truthful guard, he knows the other guard will lie, so he will point to the door that leads to captivity.
2. If you ask the lying guard, he will lie about what the truthful guard would say, also pointing to the door that leads to captivity.

In both cases, the guard you ask will point to the door that leads to captivity. Knowing this, you can choose the opposite door to find the one that leads to freedom.
</second_data>

